    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>For skin, hair and nails, marine collagen has the better case.</strong> It is
        almost entirely type I, the collagen your skin is built from, and its peptides are smaller,
        with absorption work reporting up to 1.5 times more collagen peptide in the blood than
        other animal sources. Bovine gives you type I and type III together, and usually costs
        less per gram.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source.</strong> Marine collagen is extracted from fish skin and scales. Bovine collagen comes from cow hide, bone and connective tissue.</li>
        <li><strong>Types.</strong> Fish skin collagen is roughly 90% type I, the dominant collagen in the dermis. Bovine hide gives type I with a useful share of type III.</li>
        <li><strong>Diet.</strong> Bovine collagen is a beef by-product, which rules it out for a large share of Indian households. Marine sidesteps that question entirely. Neither is vegetarian.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation.</strong> No trial has ever randomised people to marine or bovine and measured skin outcomes head to head. The absorption advantage is real in blood samples. Whether it shows up in your face is an inference, not a proven result.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

