      <h2 id="the-difference">What is the difference between marine and bovine collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        The difference is the animal and the tissue. Marine collagen is extracted from fish skin
        and scales; bovine collagen comes from cow hide, bone and connective tissue. That one fact
        drives everything that follows: the collagen types you get, the peptide size, the price,
        and whether the product is something you are willing to swallow at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some groundwork first. Collagen is the structural protein that holds the body together,
        making up roughly a third of all the protein you carry: the scaffolding of skin, the rope
        in tendons, the framework inside bone. Your body makes less of it every year from the
        mid-twenties, which is why anyone starts reading these labels
        (<a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">the skin evidence is covered here</a>). Every
        supplement worth buying is also hydrolysed, meaning the long chains have been cut
        enzymatically into short fragments called peptides. That is what makes oral collagen
        absorbable at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now the types. There are 28 known collagen types and three matter for supplementation.
        <strong>Type I</strong> is the collagen of skin, bone, tendon and the dermal tissue around
        hair follicles. <strong>Type III</strong> sits beside it in skin, blood vessels and the gut
        wall, and is proportionally more abundant in younger tissue. <strong>Type II</strong> is
        cartilage collagen, structurally different, and neither fish skin nor cow hide supplies a
        meaningful amount of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fish skin and scale collagen is close to pure type I. Bovine hide collagen is mostly type I
        with type III alongside it, in a ratio that varies by supplier and extraction method. That
        is the entire structural argument in one line: if your target is skin, marine gives you a
        cleaner match; if your target is connective tissue generally, the bovine mix is arguably
        more representative of what you are trying to support.
      </p>

