      <h2 id="absorption">Marine vs bovine collagen: which one absorbs better?</h2>

      <p>
        Marine, on the evidence available, though the margin is narrower than the marketing
        suggests. Fish collagen peptides are naturally lower in molecular weight, and studies that
        measure collagen peptides circulating in human blood after a dose consistently find more of
        them from fish-derived hydrolysates than from mammalian ones. The gap is real. What it
        means for your skin is a separate question.
      </p>

      <p>
        Start with the objection that used to sink the whole category. Intact collagen is an
        enormous molecule, around 300,000 Daltons, and your gut dismantles it into individual amino
        acids that go wherever the body happens to need them. Nothing about that is skin-specific.
      </p>

      <p>
        What changed the argument was the finding that hydrolysed collagen behaves differently.
        Iwai and colleagues demonstrated in 2005 that specific fragments, <strong>Pro-Hyp and
        Hyp-Gly</strong>, appear intact in human blood after oral collagen hydrolysate. These are
        di- and tripeptides, small enough to cross the gut wall as recognisable units rather than
        dissolving into the general amino acid pool. Current thinking is that they act partly as
        signals, reaching the dermis and prompting fibroblasts, the cells that manufacture
        collagen, to increase output.
      </p>

      <p>
        Peptide size therefore matters more than the animal on the front of the pack. Fragments
        below roughly 2,000 Daltons absorb efficiently. A well-made marine hydrolysate commonly
        sits between 500 and 2,000 Daltons. Bovine hydrolysates start from a larger, more
        cross-linked baseline and are harder to reduce that far, which is where the absorption gap
        comes from. A badly hydrolysed marine collagen will still lose to a well-hydrolysed bovine
        one, so the source is a starting condition, not a guarantee.
      </p>

