      <p>
        The 1.5 times figure needs a caveat, and you will not often see brands offer one. It was
        measured in plasma, not in skin, and it compares peptide concentrations rather than
        outcomes. Nobody has run the study that would settle this properly: the same people,
        randomised to marine or bovine at matched doses, with wrinkle depth and elasticity measured
        at twelve weeks. Until that exists, "marine absorbs better" is supportable and "marine
        works 1.5 times better" is not. I would rather say that out loud than let a
        pharmacokinetics number do work it was never designed for.
      </p>

      <h2 id="beef-question">Is bovine collagen made from beef, and does that matter in India?</h2>

      <p>
        Yes, it is. Bovine collagen is extracted from the hide, bones and connective tissue of
        cattle, which makes it a by-product of the beef industry. For a large share of Indian
        households that ends the conversation before absorption or price gets a hearing, and I
        think brands selling here have been evasive about it for too long.
      </p>

      <p>
        In my OPD this comes up more often than any question about efficacy. A patient brings in a
        tub bought online, turns it over, and asks me what "bovine" means. When I tell them, the
        tub does not go back into the bag. It is not only a religious objection either: plenty of
        patients who eat chicken and fish have never eaten beef and feel misled by a label that
        used a Latin-adjacent word for cow.
      </p>

      <p>
        A practical note on labelling. Under FSSAI rules, any packaged food containing an
        ingredient of animal origin carries the brown mark on the front, and both marine and bovine
        collagen carry it. If you are vegetarian, that mark is your answer for either product. The
        pastel packaging that dominates this category does its best to make you forget it.
      </p>

      <p>
        What about the products marketed as vegan collagen? Almost all of them are collagen
        builders rather than collagen: vitamin C, glycine and proline, sometimes bamboo silica.
        Whether that approach does anything measurable deserves its own article, and it will get
        one. Until then, the diet and sun habits that protect the collagen you already have are the
        higher-yield place to spend effort.
      </p>

      <p>
        The other bovine question I get is about prion disease, the BSE worry from the 1990s. Hides
        are classified as low-infectivity tissue, extraction uses acid and alkaline processing that
        is harsh by design, and no case has been linked to a collagen supplement. The risk is very
        low. It is not zero the way a fish scale is zero, so country of origin is worth checking.
      </p>

