      <h2 id="fish-vs-bovine-skin">Is fish collagen better than bovine collagen for skin?</h2>

      <p>
        For skin specifically, fish collagen has the better mechanistic case: closer type match,
        smaller peptides, faster absorption. But both sources have positive controlled trials
        behind them, and the performance gap between them is almost certainly narrower than the
        price gap. Anyone telling you bovine collagen does nothing for skin is overstating their
        case.
      </p>

      <p>
        The cleanest wrinkle data in this field actually comes from a bovine product. Proksch and
        colleagues randomised 114 women aged 45 to 65 to 2.5g of specific bovine collagen peptides
        or placebo for eight weeks, and measured eye wrinkle volume with a skin imaging system
        rather than a questionnaire. The treated group showed roughly a 20% reduction against
        placebo, and the effect was still measurable four weeks after they stopped. That is a small
        dose, a real measurement method, and a result that held.
      </p>

      <p>
        On the marine side, Evans and colleagues ran a twelve-week randomised, placebo-controlled
        trial of a hydrolysed marine collagen and reported improvements in wrinkle appearance and
        elasticity. The 2021 systematic review in the <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em>,
        pooling 19 randomised trials across 1,125 people, found consistent benefit for hydration,
        elasticity and wrinkle depth. It mixed marine and bovine studies together and did not
        resolve which source performs better, because the underlying trials were never designed to
        answer that.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is how I read the whole body of it. Dose and consistency explain most of the variation
        between people who see results and people who do not; source explains a smaller slice. A 5g
        bovine peptide taken every morning for three months will beat a premium marine collagen
        taken twice a week, and it will beat a 500mg marine sachet taken religiously, because 500mg
        is below anything that has ever been studied.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two adjacent cases are worth separating out. For hair, remember that the hair shaft is
        keratin, not collagen; the case for collagen rests on the dermal environment around the
        follicle, and it is weaker than the skin case. For
        joints, neither marine nor bovine hide collagen is the first choice, because cartilage runs
        on type II (<a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">the joint evidence sits here</a>).
      </p>

