      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is marine collagen better than bovine collagen for skin?</summary>
          <p>
            For skin, marine has the better case: fish skin collagen is roughly 90% type I, the
            dominant collagen in the dermis, and its peptides hydrolyse to a lower molecular weight
            that absorbs more readily. Bovine collagen also has positive skin trials behind it, so
            the difference is a margin rather than a gulf. Dose matters more than source.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is bovine collagen suitable if you do not eat beef?</summary>
          <p>
            No. Bovine collagen is extracted from cattle hide, bone and connective tissue, so it is
            a beef by-product regardless of how the label is worded. Marine collagen from fish skin
            and scales is the usual alternative, though it is not vegetarian either.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is grass fed collagen worth paying more for?</summary>
          <p>
            Not for performance. Grass fed describes the cow's diet, not the collagen's structure,
            and no study shows better absorption or better skin results from grass fed bovine
            collagen. It is a reasonable proxy for husbandry and residue standards, which is an
            ethics decision rather than a potency one.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does marine collagen taste fishy?</summary>
          <p>
            A poorly processed marine collagen does carry a marine note, and it is the most common
            reason people abandon a tub half finished. Well-deodorised marine collagen in a
            flavoured liquid shot is much easier to take daily than an unflavoured powder stirred
            into cold water. Taste decides adherence, and adherence decides results.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can you take marine and bovine collagen together?</summary>
          <p>
            You can, and there is no known interaction, but there is little reason to. You would be
            paying twice for overlapping type I collagen. Pick one source, hit a daily dose between
            2.5g and 10g, and stay consistent for at least twelve weeks.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much marine or bovine collagen should I take per day?</summary>
          <p>
            Published trials showing skin benefits used 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides
            daily, from either source. Doses below 2.5g have almost no supporting evidence.
            Collabloom delivers 8,000mg per 30ml shot, which sits in the upper part of that studied
            range.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

